  "enhanced_information": "During T cell differentiation in the thymus, T cells undergo positive selection in the cortex, where they interact with MHC molecules presented by cortical epithelial cells. T cells that do not bind to these MHC molecules undergo apoptosis within 3-4 days. This process primarily leads to the development of CD4+ and CD8+ T cells, which are the two main subsets of T cells that emerge from this selection process. The chains correctly indicate that both CD4+ and CD8+ T cells are present during this phase, confirming that the T cells are in the process of differentiating into these functional subsets. The evidence paths support the role of MHC in T cell selection but are too broad to provide specific insights into the differentiation phase being described. Therefore, the best description of the T cells during this phase of differentiation is that they are CD4+ and CD8+ T cells."
